# UI governance

These rules apply to new screens and substantial changes to existing screens.
Page-specific product decisions stay in that page's design document.

## 1. Product shell

- Use one light theme. Do not add a dark theme, a `.dark` palette, or
  `class="dark"` on `<html>`.
- Inter, loaded with `next/font/google`, is the only product face for titles,
  navigation, and money. Money uses `formatCurrency` and `tabular-nums`.
- Use the tokens in `frontend/app/globals.css` (`--background`, `--card`,
  `--foreground`, `--muted`, `--border`, `--primary`, `--success`,
  `--destructive`, `--transfer`, `--warning`, and the chart and series
  tokens). Do not invent a second palette.
- `--destructive` is a failure or destructive action. `--warning` is something
  the user must finish or fix that is not a failure. Pair a warning with
  `TriangleAlert` for a summary or `CircleAlert` for a row, `text-warning`,
  and a `border-warning/40` panel or `bg-warning/10` callout. Do not render a
  warning as destructive or as unmarked plain text.
- `--chart-1` is the main stroke and `--chart-2` is a comparison. Use
  `--series-1` through `--series-8` for several compared items. Neighbors
  contrast and each is at least 3:1 on white. A ninth item reuses
  `--series-1`, and its name still distinguishes it.
- `--primary` is a mark for the active destination, a link, or the main chart
  stroke. It does not fill the page. A category color appears only on that
  category.
- Primary destinations remain Home `/`, Accounts `/accounts`, Activity
  `/activity`, and Plan `/plan`, in that order. The phone tab bar uses those
  four.
- Settings remain in the account menu at both widths: Income, Bills, Debts,
  Plan budget, Savings, Spending targets, Categories, Connections, and
  Household. A new screen is one of the four primary destinations or a
  setting; it does not become a fifth primary destination.
- The shell breaks at 768px (`md`). From that width up, use the left sidebar.
  Below it, use the tab bar and existing safe-area padding.
- A record or form opens as a right-hand panel from `md` up and a full-screen
  sheet below it. A top-level surface uses close on desktop and back on a
  phone. A nested step uses back at both widths.
- A choice opens as a popover from `md` up and a bottom sheet below it. Choice
  lists use `Select`; dates use `DateField`.
- Use a flat light canvas and white groups with a 1px border. Leave out a
  second font, script wordmark, wallpaper, panel rail, entrance animation,
  and highlighter behind a number.

## 2. Purpose and hierarchy

- Before implementation, identify the screen's primary user question, its
  next action when it has one, and its supporting details. Briefly state that
  hierarchy in the implementation plan or a progress update. This is not a
  separate artifact or approval gate.
- Lead with the answer and the relevant action. Put analysis, healthy checks,
  and explanation after them or behind disclosure.
- Organize a screen around user tasks and decisions, not the order or nesting
  of an API response.
- When adding content to an existing screen, reassess the whole screen's
  hierarchy and length. Choose the main flow, an existing section, a
  disclosure, local tabs, or a detail surface deliberately. Do not default to
  appending another card.
- Preserve access to useful information while removing repeated copy, repeated
  status, and competing visual emphasis.
- Use one `h1`. Section titles are sentence case and smaller than the page
  title. Labels, buttons, and navigation are sentence case.

## 3. Composition and responsive behavior

- Define the desktop and mobile arrangements before implementation. Share
  data, behavior, state ownership, and reusable components while adapting
  grouping, control layout, and disclosure to the available space.
- Choose content width for the task. Bound prose and forms to a comfortable
  reading width. Let analytical views use a wider bounded layout when related
  information benefits from appearing together.
- Reduce repetition and secondary content before shrinking type or spacing.
  Use cards for meaningful groups, not every element. Do not impose a
  universal card count, chart count, or above-the-fold quota.
- On mobile, keep the primary answer and action before supporting detail. Fit
  controls and labels without horizontal page scrolling. Keep amounts, dates,
  warnings, and text readable at narrow widths and with text zoom.
- Make chart information available through touch and keyboard interaction or
  an equivalent readable data view.
- Use comfortable touch targets and visible focus. Sticky elements must not
  cover content, controls, or keyboard focus.
- Avoid unnecessary nested scrolling and mobile-only copies of the same
  feature. Preserve relevant input and selection state when resizing or
  changing a local view.
- Keep one web product. Do not introduce a separate mobile information
  architecture, native app, or PWA for responsive layout.

## 4. Navigation and progressive disclosure

- Local tabs separate distinct tasks within one destination. Filters change
  the same dataset or view. Disclosures reveal supporting detail. Panels
  support focused inspection or editing.
- The prohibition on a second navigation means a competing app-level
  navigation. It does not prohibit useful local tabs.
- Preserve relevant state across local tabs and other local view changes.
- Critical errors, required actions, and missing inputs that make a result
  invalid remain discoverable while supporting details are collapsed.
- A top-level destination does not show a back control. Back is for a nested
  flow.

## 5. Warnings and result reliability

- Give each issue one primary presentation within a view. Rank issues by their
  effect on the user's task and the reliability of the result.
- Summarize additional issues with a count and access to details. Keep healthy
  checks quiet. Repeat a warning only where it is needed to interpret a nearby
  result or action.
- Keep material financial qualifications visible. Simplifying a page must not
  hide a shortfall, missing input, stale source, exclusion, or assumption that
  changes how a result should be understood.
- Retain the semantic colors and warning icons in section 1. Color never
  carries the meaning alone.

## 6. Charts and metrics

- Each chart or prominent metric answers a distinct user question. Use a
  concise summary when a visualization adds little.
- Keep units, timeframes, and material qualifications beside the result.
  Distinguish actual values, forecasts, and temporary scenarios.
- Essential information does not depend on hover or color. Tooltips and hover
  labels do not cover chart text. A donut shows the inspected item in its
  center instead of covering the chart with a floating tooltip.
- Preserve a series' identity across related charts and lists. Do not invent
  precision or intermediate values the application does not provide.

## 7. Actions and states

- When a view has an actionable purpose, give it one clearly dominant action
  and keep other actions quieter. Do not manufacture an action for a
  read-only view.
- Keep screen-level actions predictably associated with the page heading or
  primary result, and panel actions in the panel header. A contextual next
  action may sit beside the result it addresses. Preserve that relationship
  across widths, but allow controls to wrap or stack instead of forcing an
  awkward phone title row.
- Related buttons use the shared `buttonVariants` sizes, not hand-sized pills.
  Repeated row actions align when that improves scanning.
- A save, sync, and destructive action each says what happened. A destructive
  action names the thing it removes and asks first.
- Every view has an explicit loading, empty, and error state, plus a stale
  state when data can be out of date. Empty copy says what is missing and
  offers the action that fills it. An error says what failed and how to retry.
  Stale data remains visible and says whether refresh is running or failed.

## 8. Accessibility

- Use semantic elements: `button`, `a`, `nav`, `main`, headings, `label`, and
  lists. A clickable `div` is not a control.
- Every control has an accessible name. A placeholder is not that name.
  Icon-only actions still have a name.
- Tab reaches every control. Enter or Space activates it. Escape closes the
  top panel or popover while leaving the underlying screen in place. Focus
  returns to the control that opened it.
- Keep focus visible on the light canvas. Do not remove the outline without a
  visible replacement.
- Text, icons, and the accent remain readable on `#f3f2ee` and white. Meta text
  is secondary information, not a required label.
- Primary phone controls and tab items have a target of at least 44px.
- Pair income, spending, transfer, warning, and error colors with text or an
  icon.
- Honor `prefers-reduced-motion`. Motion is not required to use a panel,
  sheet, chart, or control.
- A custom widget exposes its name, role, and value. A panel is a dialog. A
  popover names itself. A tab exposes selection. A toggle uses `aria-pressed`
  or `aria-expanded`. A switch uses `role="switch"` and `aria-checked`, with
  On or Off beside the track.

## 9. Decisions and UX verification

- Make routine, reversible layout choices that follow these rules without
  asking for approval.
- Ask Zach when an unresolved choice materially changes the user workflow,
  product behavior, scope, or adds a dependency. Offer concise options and a
  recommendation. Spacing and ordinary component arrangement do not require
  approval.
- A review for a new screen or substantial UI change briefly records what the
  user sees and can do first, what is secondary and how it is reached, how the
  layout adapts on mobile, and which visual or interaction checks await Zach.
- Zach remains the manual visual, usability, and end-to-end tester. Agents run
  appropriate compilation, lint, formatting, and focused behavior checks for
  implementation changes. A passing build does not establish visual
  usability. Do not make automated browser QA or a large checklist mandatory
  for every small change.
